    "['local-address [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 65008', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nrouter bgp 65008\\nneighbor RR update-source Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, you would typically need to first configure the BGP process with the local AS number (which I've assumed as 65008 based on the peer AS in the Juniper config). The `update-source Loopback0` command is the Cisco equivalent to Juniper's `local-address`, assuming that 5.5.5.5 is configured on Loopback0 in the Cisco configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp 65008'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor RR update-source Loopback0', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nrouter bgp 65008\\nneighbor RR update-source Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, you would typically need to first configure the BGP process with the local AS number (which I've assumed as 65008 based on the peer AS in the Juniper config). The `update-source Loopback0` command is the Cisco equivalent to Juniper's `local-address`, assuming that 5.5.5.5 is configured on Loopback0 in the Cisco configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor RR update-source Loopback0'}]]": 1,
    "['neighbor [parameter2] peer-as [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 65008', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the given Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 65008\\n neighbor 172.16.2.1 remote-as 65008\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, you need to first specify the local AS number (65008 in this case, which we can infer from the peer-as value in the Juniper config) in the \"router bgp\" statement before configuring the neighbor. The Juniper configuration shows this is an external BGP peer (type external) with the same AS number as the local router.', 'trans_template': 'router bgp 65008'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 172.16.2.1 remote-as 65008', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the given Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 65008\\n neighbor 172.16.2.1 remote-as 65008\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, you need to first specify the local AS number (65008 in this case, which we can infer from the peer-as value in the Juniper config) in the \"router bgp\" statement before configuring the neighbor. The Juniper configuration shows this is an external BGP peer (type external) with the same AS number as the local router.', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 172.16.2.1 remote-as 65008'}]]": 1,
    "['neighbor [parameter2] peer-as [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 65008', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP neighbor configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 65008\\n neighbor 172.16.4.2 remote-as 65008\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco:\\n1. The BGP process is initiated with \"router bgp [local-AS-number]\"\\n2. Each neighbor is configured with \"neighbor [IP] remote-as [peer-AS-number]\"\\n3. Unlike Juniper where the local AS is specified in the neighbor configuration, Cisco requires it in the initial \"router bgp\" statement', 'trans_template': 'router bgp 65008'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 172.16.4.2 remote-as 65008', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP neighbor configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 65008\\n neighbor 172.16.4.2 remote-as 65008\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco:\\n1. The BGP process is initiated with \"router bgp [local-AS-number]\"\\n2. Each neighbor is configured with \"neighbor [IP] remote-as [peer-AS-number]\"\\n3. Unlike Juniper where the local AS is specified in the neighbor configuration, Cisco requires it in the initial \"router bgp\" statement', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 172.16.4.2 remote-as 65008'}]]": 1,
    "['neighbor [parameter2] peer-as [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 65008', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco IOS command for the given Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 65008\\n neighbor 172.16.5.2 remote-as 65008\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, you would first need to enter the BGP configuration mode with the local AS number (65008 in this case, as inferred from the peer-as value), then configure the neighbor with the remote AS number. The Juniper configuration doesn't explicitly show the local AS number, but in Cisco, this must be specified when entering BGP configuration mode.\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp 65008'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 172.16.5.2 remote-as 65008', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco IOS command for the given Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 65008\\n neighbor 172.16.5.2 remote-as 65008\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, you would first need to enter the BGP configuration mode with the local AS number (65008 in this case, as inferred from the peer-as value), then configure the neighbor with the remote AS number. The Juniper configuration doesn't explicitly show the local AS number, but in Cisco, this must be specified when entering BGP configuration mode.\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 172.16.5.2 remote-as 65008'}]]": 1,
