    "['set policy-options policy-statement [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-map export-direct permit 10', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'route-map export-direct permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco configuration for the Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map export-direct permit 10\\n match protocol connected\\n##\\nThe Juniper `policy-statement export-direct term 1 from protocol direct` matches directly connected routes, which in Cisco is accomplished with `match protocol connected`. The `then accept` action in Juniper corresponds to the `permit` action in Cisco\\'s route-map. The \"10\" is a sequence number, similar to how \"term 1\" identifies the policy term in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'route-map export-direct permit 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'match protocol connected', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'match protocol connected', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco configuration for the Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map export-direct permit 10\\n match protocol connected\\n##\\nThe Juniper `policy-statement export-direct term 1 from protocol direct` matches directly connected routes, which in Cisco is accomplished with `match protocol connected`. The `then accept` action in Juniper corresponds to the `permit` action in Cisco\\'s route-map. The \"10\" is a sequence number, similar to how \"term 1\" identifies the policy term in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'match protocol connected'}]]": 1,
    "['term [parameter2] from protocol [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-map export-direct permit 10', 'parent_command': ['route-map export-direct permit 10'], 'root': 'route-map export-direct permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map export-direct permit 10\\n match protocol connected\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, `policy-statement export-direct term 1` translates to `route-map export-direct permit 10` in Cisco\\n- `from protocol direct` in Juniper becomes `match protocol connected` in Cisco (Juniper\\'s \"direct\" protocol is equivalent to Cisco\\'s \"connected\" routes)\\n- The `then accept` is implied by the `permit` action in the route-map statement', 'trans_template': 'route-map export-direct permit 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'match protocol connected', 'parent_command': ['route-map export-direct permit 10'], 'root': 'route-map export-direct permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map export-direct permit 10\\n match protocol connected\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, `policy-statement export-direct term 1` translates to `route-map export-direct permit 10` in Cisco\\n- `from protocol direct` in Juniper becomes `match protocol connected` in Cisco (Juniper\\'s \"direct\" protocol is equivalent to Cisco\\'s \"connected\" routes)\\n- The `then accept` is implied by the `permit` action in the route-map statement', 'trans_template': 'match protocol connected'}]]": 1,
